  "body": {
    "id": "prop:three-horizon-smooth-nonidentification-transfer",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "Suppose \\(0<\\eta\\leq1/4\\).  Apply the same sufficiently narrow separated bump kernels to the two full laws \\(q^-_{ihj}\\) and \\(q^+_{ihj}\\) in \\(\\mathfrak E_3\\).  The resulting two bounded-Lipschitz full laws satisfy the maintained event-order and terminal-monotonicity restrictions, have identical smoothed control and treated within-arm laws, and induce the same observable distribution under the common randomized treatment and full-horizon censoring mechanisms.  Their values of \\(\\ell_\\star\\) remain \\(-1/20\\) and \\(1/4\\).  Hence every maintained smooth causal class containing both smoothed laws is non-point-identified for \\(\\ell_\\star\\), without any implication that the marked-capacity image is sharp in a larger nonmonotone class.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\eta",
      "\\mathfrak E_3",
      "q^-_{ihj}",
      "q^+_{ihj}",
      "\\ell_\\star"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:primitive-three-horizon-exhibit",
      "def:cross-cohort-direction",
      "def:target-domain",
      "prop:strict-cross-cohort-support",
      "ass:consistency"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Fix one separated bump system from the strict-support proposition and use it under both signs.  Let \\(H_{i,\\delta}\\) be the death bump centered at \\(d_i^\\star\\), let \\(J_{j,\\delta}\\) be the readmission bump centered at \\(r_j^\\star\\), and retain point masses for the \\(\\infty\\) categories.  Under either sign, the smoothed control within-arm law is\n\\[\n \\mu_{0,\\delta}^{\\pm}\n =\\sum_i\\left(\\sum_{h,j}q^\\pm_{ihj}\\right)\n   (H_{i,\\delta}\\otimes\\delta_\\infty)\n =\\sum_i\\frac14(H_{i,\\delta}\\otimes\\delta_\\infty),                 \\tag{44}\n\\]\nso it is common to the two laws.  For the treated arm, the conditional kernel for \\((D(1),R(1))\\) depends only on \\((h,j)\\), even when the \\(D(0)\\) and \\(D(1)\\) bump draws are shared in an equal-death category.  Therefore\n\\[\n \\mu_{1,\\delta}^{\\pm}\n =\\sum_{h,j}\\left(\\sum_iq^\\pm_{ihj}\\right)\n   (H_{h,\\delta}\\otimes J_{j,\\delta})\n =\\sum_{h,j}\\mu_{1,hj}^\\star\n   (H_{h,\\delta}\\otimes J_{j,\\delta}),                              \\tag{45}\n\\]\nwhich is also common.  These equalities prove equality of the complete smoothed within-arm laws, not merely equality of their death margins.\n\nThe strict-support proposition proves that the separated kernels preserve \\(D(0)\\leq D(1)\\) and \\(R(z)<D(z)\\) whenever \\(R(z)<\\infty\\), and that the finite-part within-arm densities are bounded and Lipschitz.  Use under both signs the same degenerate covariate, independent treatment with probability \\(1/2\\), and \\(C=\\tau=3\\).  Consistency then gives, for every measurable observed-path set \\(A\\),\n\\[\n \\Pr_{\\pm,\\delta}(O\\in A)\n =\\frac12\\sum_{z=0}^1\n   \\int\\mathbf 1\\{\\mathcal O(z,d,r,3)\\in A\\}\\,\n          d\\mu_{z,\\delta}^\\star(d,r),                                \\tag{46}\n\\]\nwhere (44)--(45) show that the right side is independent of the sign.  Thus the two smooth laws are observationally equivalent.  The bump supports cross none of the death or readmission thresholds \\(1,2,3\\); the survivor denominators and the three numerators defining \\(\\ell_\\star\\) are consequently unchanged.  Since \\(0<\\eta\\leq1/4\\) keeps those coordinates in \\(\\mathcal H_\\eta\\), the two smooth laws retain the values \\(-1/20\\) and \\(1/4\\).\n\nAny maintained smooth causal class containing both laws therefore has, at the common observable distribution (46), an identified set for \\(\\ell_\\star\\) containing two values separated by \\(3/10\\).  This proves non-point-identification.  It is only a two-witness containment argument and does not characterize the identified set after removal of terminal monotonicity.",
    "justification": "The common-kernel construction separately certifies observational equivalence and distinct signed supports inside the bounded-Lipschitz regime.",
    "gap": "Atomic observational equivalence cannot be transferred to a smooth model by assertion; equality of both smoothed within-arm laws is proved after accounting for shared death draws.",
    "consumer": "Users retaining the smoothness audit can invoke the separated-bump pair for nonidentification, without treating it as an enlarged-class sharpness theorem.",
